WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.747
This slide shows why parentheses are part of the meaning of a query.

00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:07.597
We have two versions of a rule.

00:00:07.597 --> 00:00:14.297
In the first version, because AND is evaluated before OR, the consent condition applies only to the Kidney

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:20.382
Disease branch. That means anyone who satisfies the age condition can qualify even when consent is false.

00:00:20.382 --> 00:00:27.072
In the second version, parentheses group the age condition and the Kidney Disease condition together.

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:30.467
Then the consent requirement is applied to the whole group.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:37.492
Now consent is required regardless of which alternative caused the patient to qualify.

00:00:37.492 --> 00:00:42.932
The patient Lisa Ray is a useful boundary example because she is older than sixty-five but has false

00:00:42.932 --> 00:00:48.867
consent. In the unparenthesized rule, her age is enough to qualify.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:52.967
In the parenthesized rule, she fails because consent is required for both alternatives.

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:55.787
This is not a tiny formatting difference.

00:00:55.787 --> 00:00:58.797
Imagine this were a real outreach campaign.

00:00:58.797 --> 00:01:03.482
The first query could include people who explicitly declined contact.

00:01:03.482 --> 00:01:09.727
That could create a privacy or compliance problem even though the SQL contains no syntax error.

00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:16.832
When you see a rule such as “A or B, but only when C is true,” write it

00:01:16.832 --> 00:01:22.407
as a grouped expression: the A-or-B portion belongs together, and then C applies to that whole decision.

00:01:22.407 --> 00:01:25.737
Run both versions and compare the output.

00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:31.155
Seeing one record move in or out makes the precedence rule much easier to remember.
